"""
=======================================================================================
 MEDICONNECT INDIA - Unified Medical Services Platform
=======================================================================================
A single-file Flask application implementing:

  Roles: Patient, Doctor, Hospital, Laboratory, Ambulance Provider, Medical Store / Pharmacy, Admin

  Features:
   - Mandatory registration with Mobile/Email OTP verification
   - Degree / License / Registration document upload for Doctor, Hospital, Lab, Medical Store
   - Admin verification queue (documents must be approved before a provider goes live, including medical stores)
   - Hospital: register, list services, submit/update price quotes, manage bookings,
               manage doctors, mark Ayushman Bharat (govt scheme) participation
   - Doctor: profile with degree shown publicly, consultation booking + fee payment,
             audio/video consultation calls with the patient
   - Lab: list tests + prices, home sample collection toggle, upload reports (PDF)
   - Ambulance: register vehicle, type, live GPS location, receive bookings
   - Patient: compare hospital prices across India ("bid"/lowest price search),
              book doctor consultation (+ audio/video call), book lab home-collection,
              book ambulance with live map tracking, check Ayushman Bharat eligibility,
              pay (simulated gateway), get notifications, download lab reports
   - Notifications (in-app) for booking confirmed, reminders, technician arriving,
     ambulance assigned, payment success, report available, booking cancelled
   - Platform commission: MediConnect reserves 15% of every paid booking as revenue
   - Basic security: hashed passwords, session-based auth, role-based access control,
     server-side file-type/size checks, simple audit log table

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 HONEST NOTES ON WHAT IS SIMULATED (clearly marked with "# TODO PRODUCTION:")
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 1. OTP delivery: There is no SMS/Email account configured, so the OTP is printed to
    the console/terminal so you can test the flow end-to-end. Plug in Twilio/MSG91.
 2. Payment gateway: A mock "Payment Confirmation" screen simulates success/failure.
    Plug in Razorpay/Stripe/PayU checkout where marked.
 3. Ambulance live-tracking map: uses Google Maps JS API. You must set the
    GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY environment variable for it to render (see below). Pickup-point
    selection uses free OpenStreetMap/Leaflet instead, so that part needs no key.
 4. Audio/Video calls: real, working, peer-to-peer WebRTC (camera/mic go directly
    between patient and doctor's browsers - MediConnect's server never sees or stores
    the call media). Signaling (the initial "handshake" that lets two browsers find
    each other) is done via simple polling through this app's own database, so no
    third-party calling service or API key is required. Because it's plain WebRTC
    with only a public STUN server, calls may fail to connect on some strict
    corporate/mobile networks - a production deployment would add a TURN server
    (e.g. Twilio TURN, coturn) as a fallback relay.
 5. Encryption at rest / full HIPAA-DPDP compliance / real audit-grade logging is a
    much larger infrastructure project (KMS, encrypted volumes, access reviews).
    This app hashes passwords, restricts uploads, and logs key actions to an
    `audit_log` table as a foundation - not a compliance certification.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 HOW TO RUN (VS Code)
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 1. pip install flask
 2. python app.py
 3. Open http://127.0.0.1:5000 in your browser
 4. Watch the terminal for OTP codes when registering (simulated SMS/Email)
 5. Default admin login is created automatically:
        mobile: 9999999999   password: Admin@123
    (Change this immediately in a real deployment.)
 6. (Optional, for the ambulance live-map) set an environment variable before running:
        Windows (cmd):  set GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY=your_key_here
        Windows (ps):   $env:GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY="your_key_here"
        macOS/Linux:    export GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY=your_key_here
    Without it, everything else (including calls, bookings, payments) still works;
    only the live ambulance map screen will show a "not configured" notice.

 The database (medi_connect.db) and an /uploads folder are created automatically
 next to this file the first time you run it.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 TRY THE FULL FLOW (recommended first run)
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 1. Register as a Hospital (or Doctor/Lab/Ambulance) -> verify the OTP printed in
    this terminal -> you land on your provider dashboard.
 2. Log out, log in as Admin (9999999999 / Admin@123) -> Dashboard -> approve the
    pending hospital/doctor/lab/ambulance (this is the document/degree check).
 3. Log out, register as a Patient -> verify OTP -> browse "Compare Hospitals" /
    "Find Doctors" / "Book Lab Test" / "Book Ambulance" -> book something -> pay
    on the mock payment screen -> see it confirmed on your dashboard and check
    the Notifications bell.
 4. To try a call: book + pay a Doctor consultation as a Patient, then open the
    same booking in two browser windows (one logged in as the Patient, one as
    the Doctor) and click "Video Call" / "Audio Call" on either side.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 HANDLING MORE USERS / GOING LIVE
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 `app.run(debug=True, threaded=True)` at the bottom of this file is fine for
 testing and demos. For real public traffic:
   - Turn off debug mode.
   - Run behind a real WSGI server, e.g.:  gunicorn -w 4 -b 0.0.0.0:8000 app:app
   - Put Nginx in front of it.
   - Move from SQLite to PostgreSQL once you have concurrent writes from many
     hospitals/labs at once (SQLite is fine for a demo/small pilot, not for
     heavy concurrent traffic).
=======================================================================================
